<BaseLayout title="Ripple Links — Privacy Policy" description="How Ripple Links handles game progress, non-personalised Google ads, advertising consent choices and the Remove Ads purchase.">
  <section class="privacy shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Ripple Links · Privacy</p>
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="lede">How Pluto Night Labs Ltd handles information relating to <em>Ripple Links</em>.</p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated: 18 November 2026</p>

    <div class="summary">
      <p><strong>In short:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Ripple Links has no accounts and no analytics. Your progress and settings stay on your iPhone.</li>
        <li>The free version shows occasional full-screen ads from Google between ponds. They are <strong>non-personalised</strong>: they are not chosen from a profile of you. Google's ad service still uses some device and usage information to show, cap and measure them. In the UK, EEA and Switzerland we ask for your choice first.</li>
        <li>If you buy <em>Remove Ads</em>, Apple handles the payment and the game stops requesting ads.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="privacy-copy">
      <h2>Who we are</h2>
      <p>Ripple Links is made by Pluto Night Labs Ltd (company number 17485329), registered in England and Wales at 128 City Road, London EC1V 2NX, United Kingdom. For anything about this policy or your data, email <a href={`mailto:${site.supportEmail}`}>{site.supportEmail}</a>.</p>

      <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
      <p>The game saves which ponds you have restored and your Music, Sounds and Haptics settings in the app's own storage on your iPhone. We do not receive this information, and it is not sent to us or anyone else. Deleting the app deletes it.</p>
      <p>Ripple Links has no sign-in, no account, no leaderboards and no analytics or crash-reporting service of our own. You can play offline.</p>

      <h2>Advertising (free version)</h2>
      <p>The free version uses Google's <strong>Google Mobile Ads SDK</strong> (AdMob) to show a full-screen ad between ponds. Ads appear at most after every third pond from Pond 6, and no more than three times in one session. The game does not show ads while the in-game or system <em>Reduce Motion</em> setting is on.</p>
      <p>The game asks Google for <strong>non-personalised ads only</strong>, for every player. These ads are chosen from the context, such as the fact that you are playing a puzzle game, and your general area (city level). They are not chosen from your interests or browsing across other apps. Google still uses device identifiers to limit how often you see the same ad, and to produce combined reports.</p>
      <p>When an ad is requested, Google may collect and process information such as:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Device identifiers</strong>: for example an identifier scoped to the app or developer. The advertising identifier (IDFA) is only available if you give an app permission to track, and Ripple Links does not ask for that permission.</li>
        <li><strong>Approximate location</strong>, worked out from your IP address.</li>
        <li><strong>Advertising data</strong>, such as which ads you have seen or tapped.</li>
        <li><strong>Product interaction and performance data</strong>, such as app launches, taps on ads, and app performance information.</li>
        <li><strong>Crash logs</strong> relating to the ad software.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Google uses this information to show and measure ads, as described in its own policies. Google acts under its own privacy policy: <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Privacy Policy</a>. To see how Google uses information from apps that use its services, read <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">How Google uses information from sites or apps that use our services</a>.</p>

      <h2>Your consent choices (UK, EEA and Switzerland)</h2>
      <p>If you are in the UK, the European Economic Area or Switzerland, the game uses Google's consent tool (the User Messaging Platform) to ask for your choices before any ad is requested. The game only loads ads when that tool says it may. Whatever you choose, the game never asks for personalised ads. Depending on your choices, Google may show non-personalised ads or more limited ads.</p>
      <p><strong>To change your choice later,</strong> open <em>Settings</em> in the game (the gear button), go to <em>Advertising privacy</em> and tap <em>Privacy choices</em>. This button appears whenever your region requires it.</p>
      <p>Where the law of your region calls for it, Google's consent tool may also show other privacy messages, such as US state privacy options.</p>

      <h2>Tracking</h2>
      <p>Ripple Links does not track you. It does not ask for permission to track you across other companies' apps and websites, it does not access your device's advertising identifier, and it only requests non-personalised ads. We do not sell your personal information, and we do not combine game data with data from other sources.</p>

      <h2>Purchases</h2>
      <p><em>Remove Ads</em> is a one-off in-app purchase handled entirely by Apple through the App Store. We never see your payment details, name or Apple ID. The game only checks with Apple whether this device's Apple ID owns Remove Ads. When it does, the game stops requesting ads. <em>Restore Purchases</em> in Settings asks Apple to check again.</p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>Ripple Links is a general-audience puzzle game and is not directed at children. We do not knowingly collect personal information from children. If you believe a child has used the free version and you have concerns, contact us.</p>

      <h2>Legal basis and your rights (UK and EEA)</h2>
      <p>We do not hold personal data about you ourselves. For advertising, the legal basis is the consent you give in Google's consent tool, where your region requires consent. Google also relies on its own legal bases, as set out in its policy. You can withdraw or change consent at any time under <em>Advertising privacy</em> in Settings.</p>
      <p>You can ask Google about the data it processes using the tools in its privacy policy. You can also complain to your data protection authority. In the UK, that is the Information Commissioner's Office: <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ico.org.uk</a>.</p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>If the game changes how it handles data, we will update this page and the date above before the update reaches the App Store.</p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p><a href={`mailto:${site.supportEmail}`}>{site.supportEmail}</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

